ab6-9245-89a7a070f017" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495218 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495280 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495197 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1061 616958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aebf2791-ae4f-5ea2-8312-994b316164b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495238 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495278 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1061 616951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b7fc4646-02d5-59f6-896d-aa9560955608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186495046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1031 "Working With Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1033 6818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495275 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "04654553-f8da-5ec9-a27d-ee5bae447da5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1064 "For every unit price in \"dollars per item\", there is also a **reciprocal unit price** in \"items per dollar\".\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells fabric for $\\[math]2$ per yard. Taking the reciprocal, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\,\\textrm{dollars per yard} \\quad\\rightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2}  \\,\\textrm{yards per dollar} .\n$$\n\nSo, if we have $\\[/math]1,$ then we can buy $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of a yard of fabric." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1065 3106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "18f05709-44a6-59bc-962c-dbf491ad7501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495278 #edb/ref 1067 "Reciprocal Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1064 "When we compare prices, it is often helpful to know the *unit price*. Knowing it helps us:\n\n- compare different products,\n\n- determine the total cost for a given amount,\n\n- decide which option is the better deal.\n\nFor example, suppose Lena wants to buy some apples. The price of apples at a nearby market is $\\[math]1.20/\\mathrm{lb}.$ How much would Lena pay for $4$ pounds of apples?\n\nThe unit price is $\\[/math]1.20$ per pound.  \n\nSince Lena is buying $4$ pounds, we multiply the unit price by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.20\\times 4 & =4.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Lena would pay $\\[math]4.80$ for $4$ pounds of apples.\n\nWhenever we know the unit price and the quantity, we can find the total cost by multiplying:\n\n$$\n\\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Unit Price} \\times \\text{Quantity}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1065 15086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "55a58a08-8164-5352-9b75-43c62ba6ecc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495280 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1064 "Unit prices can be used to quantify how good of a deal a particular purchase is.\n\nFor example, suppose that a store sells two packs of shirts:\n\n- $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12$\n\n- $5$ shirts for $\\[/math]15$\n\nTo determine which pack is the better deal, we can compare their unit prices.\n\n- For the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n12 \\div 3 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- For the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ dividing the total cost by the total number of shirts gives\n\n$$\n15 \\div 5 = 3.\n$$\n\nSo, the unit price is $\\[/math]3.$\n\nNow we interpret the unit prices in the context of the problem.\n\n- In the pack of $3$ shirts for $\\[math]12,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]4.$\n\n- In the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15,$ each shirt costs $\\[/math]3.$\n\nBased on the above, we conclude that the pack of $5$ shirts for $\\[math]15$ is the better deal because it has a lower unit price (meaning that each shirt costs less)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1065 15097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "be64d01c-4eb7-5332-b26c-97583b224a56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495281 #edb/ref 1067 "Determining the Best Deal by Computing Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186514507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186514510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186514521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186514525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186514548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1045 "Working With Unit Prices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186514548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186495275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513522 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "cb8ff5bb-d59c-5914-bb61-57ac943c0155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513542 #edb/ref 1080 "$A$ and $C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513542 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513542 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "052a5fc9-a08a-5e7c-b988-4b6314c92427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513572 #edb/ref 1080 "$$4.94$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513572 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513572 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10245252-29bb-5324-a581-26085a39fe28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513579 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186513579 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592